.TH MALLOC 3
.SH NAME
malloc, free, realloc, calloc, aligned_alloc \- main memory allocator
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B #include <stdlib.h>
.PP
.B void *malloc(size_t size);
.PP
.B void free(void *ptr);
.PP
.B void *realloc(void *ptr, size_t size);
.PP
.B void *calloc(size_t nelem, size_t elsize);
.PP
.B void *aligned_alloc(size_t alignment, size_t size);
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Malloc
and
.I free
provide a simple general-purpose memory allocation package.
.I Malloc
returns a pointer to a block of at least
.I size
bytes beginning on a word boundary.
.PP
The argument to
.I free
is a pointer to a block previously allocated by
.IR malloc ;
this space is made available for further allocation,
but its contents are left undisturbed.
.B Note:
a null
.I ptr
is accepted and does nothing, which is C11 sec 7.22.3.3.  v7 would have walked
the arena from a wild address.
.PP
Needless to say, grave disorder will result if the space
assigned by
.I malloc
is overrun or if some random number is handed to
.IR free .
.PP
.I Malloc
allocates the first big enough contiguous reach of
free space
found in a circular search from the last
block allocated or freed,
coalescing adjacent free blocks as it searches.
It calls
.I sbrk
(see
.IR break (2))
to get more memory from the system when there is no
suitable space already free.
.PP
.I Realloc
changes the size of the block pointed to by
.I ptr
to
.I size
bytes and returns a pointer to the (possibly moved)
block.
The contents will be unchanged up to the
lesser of the new and old sizes.
.B Note:
a null
.I ptr
makes it a
.IR malloc ,
per C11 sec 7.22.3.5.
.PP
.I Realloc
also works if
.I ptr
points to a block freed since the last call of
.I malloc, realloc
or
.IR calloc ;
thus sequences of
.I free, malloc
and
.I realloc
can exploit the search strategy of
.I malloc
to do storage compaction.
.PP
.I Calloc
allocates space for
an array of
.I nelem
elements of size
.I elsize.
The space is initialized to zeros.
.B Note:
the product is checked for overflow, which v7 did not do.
.PP
.B Note:
.I aligned_alloc
is C11 and had no v7 ancestor.  It is
.I malloc
with its
.I alignment
argument checked.
.B An alignment wider than a word is not supported:
every allocation is already word-aligned, and there is no unit finer than a
word to align to, so the routine accepts an alignment of one word or less and
refuses anything else.
.PP
Each of the allocation routines returns a pointer
to space suitably aligned (after possible pointer coercion)
for storage of any type of object.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
.I Malloc, realloc, calloc
and
.I aligned_alloc
return a null pointer (0) if there is no available memory
or if the arena has been detectably corrupted by storing outside the bounds
of a block.
.PP
.B Note:
v7 said
.I malloc
"may be recompiled to check the arena very stringently on every transaction; see
the source code."  That
.B #ifdef debug
scaffolding is
.I gone,
not merely compiled out.  Its one structural trace is in the free-list search,
where two nested tests whose else branch was an assertion collapse into a single
condition.
.SH BUGS
When
.I realloc
returns 0,
the block
pointed to by
.I ptr
may be destroyed.
.SH BESM-6 NOTES
.SS Where the BUSY bit lives
This is the one change to the algorithm, and it is forced.
.PP
v7 packs the flag into
.B bit 0
of a block's link, which is free on a byte-addressed machine because its list
cell is two bytes wide there and every block is even.
.B Here a block is exactly one word and an address is a word index,
so adjacent blocks differ by 1 and bit 0 is a
.I significant
address bit \- setting it would name the next block.
.PP
The flag moves
.I above
the address instead, to
.BR "bit 16" :
a regular (non-fat) pointer carries its 15-bit word address in bits 15..1 with
bits 48..16 zero, so bit 16 is free, one past the top of the address space, and
it sits far below the bit-48 marker that would make a marked link look like a fat
.BR "char *" .
.PP
Nothing else in the algorithm notices \- v7 already clears the flag before every
dereference and every comparison \- and the casts it takes are free, pointer and
integer being one word each.
.SS The break is granted a page at a time
So the growth chunk and the step the allocator backs off by when refused are both
a page (1024 words).  Anything smaller asks for exactly what was just given, or
exactly what was just refused.
.PP
.IR Sbrk (2)
fails with
.BR NULL ,
not v7's
.BR "(char *)\-1" ,
which would have to be fabricated out of an integer;
.I malloc
tests for NULL.
.SS A size is checked before it becomes a word count
A
.B size_t
is 48 bits wide and a word address is 15, so an unchecked byte size would wrap
when divided into words.  There is an up-front guard for it.
.PP
Note also that
.IR calloc 's
overflow guard must
.I not
be written
.BR "(size_t)\-1" ,
which on this machine is 2**41\-1 rather than 2**48\-1: a signed-to-unsigned
conversion is a bare copy of a 41-bit pattern, not an adjustment by 2**48.
.SS realloc's two failure returns are kept apart
v7 writes them as one
.BR "return (char *)q" .
Casting a
.I null
word pointer to a fat one produces a pointer with the bit-48 marker and a byte
offset set over a zero address \- a null that is not a zero word.  The failure
path returns a plain NULL instead.
.SS This is not the c-compiler's allocator
That one claims the whole span from
.I end
to the stack on first use, which would defeat the kernel's demand-grown break and
leave nothing between the heap and the stack.
.SH FILES
share/besm6/lib/libc.a
.br
lib/libc/gen/malloc.c, calloc.c, aligned_alloc.c
.SH "SEE ALSO"
break(2), end(3)
